WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, episode 35. I'm Patrick in Vancouver. Today is July 17th,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:13.860
2026. From starbase to orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown. Raptor ignition failure on flight 13

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:20.060
forces a rapid retest cycle that tests how quickly starbase can iterate on engine start reliability.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:28.260
Elon Musk stated the next Starship attempt would occur in a few days after the T-0 abort on flight 13.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.040
The abort occurred when multiple Raptor engines on the super heavy booster failed to start.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.580
Musk posted that the issue was isolated to engine start sequencing rather than a broader hardware fault.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.900
The flight had been scheduled from starbase in South Texas. This development continues the Starship

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.020
flight test campaign that was last covered on July 16, where the central open questions remain

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:59.160
full rapid reuse cadence and orbital refueling timeline. Observers will watch whether the fix

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.520
allows a turnaround within the stated window. While starbase works the engine start fix,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.060
the rest of the Falcon and Stars shield manifest continues without pause. From an engineering

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:16.320
standpoint, the T-0 abort on flight 13 centered on Raptor engines failing to reach ignition after

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.060
the hold-down clamps released. Engine start requires precise sequencing of propellant valves,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.480
igniter energy, and chamber pressure rise before the vehicle can lift. Any deviation in valve timing

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.080
or residual gas temperature can prevent the flame front from stabilizing, triggering the onboard

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.240
abort logic that shuts down the remaining engines. The design choice to release clamps only after

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.540
a confirmed start command trades a small window of ground hold time for the ability to abort

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.420
cleanly if ignition does not occur. That trade-off keeps the booster on the pad rather than

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.060
allowing a partial lift off that would demand a more complex flight termination system.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:02.280
The rapid turnaround musk described therefore hinges on whether the start sequence telemetry points

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.800
to a software timing fix or a hardware adjustment such as igniter energy or purge flow.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:14.080
Either path collapses the idiot index of the start transient by reducing the number of ground

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.620
tests needed to certify the next attempt. The next static fire will reveal whether the fix

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.340
restores the full start reliability demonstrated on earlier flights or whether a deeper valve or

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:31.960
sensor change is required before flight 14. Raptor production rate versus starship demand remains

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:37.720
the tracked open question for the engine program. A software only resolution would preserve

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.680
the current production tempo while a hardware change could temporarily slow the cadence of

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.820
completed engines available for stacking. The same power scaling constraints that affect

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:55.780
starship engines also shape the compute side of the musk stack. EXAI acquired APR energy for more

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.880
than one billion dollars to secure mobile power generation capacity. The deal brings the colossus

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:08.080
super cluster power supply above one gigawatt. The turbines are intended to support rapid scaling

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.680
of training and inference clusters. The acquisition bypasses longer lead utility infrastructure timelines.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.700
Power availability remains a central constraint for further cluster expansion.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.960
EXAI and colossus sit on the tracked musk compute stack whose open questions include colossus power

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.940
and next cluster scale. Today's purchase directly addresses the power bottle neck that has limited

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:39.380
training run growth. With the physical power layer secured, EXAI is also shipping new software

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:47.300
to developers. EXAI released GROC 4.5 with updated inference optimizations and budget tier pricing.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.500
The model integrates directly into the cursor code editor for developer workflows.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.060
Release notes highlight improvements in reasoning speed and context handling. The update expands

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.400
access for users already running GROC instances inside cursor. Distribution through the editor

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:11.200
places the model in front of an active developer audience. Cursor remains the tracked distribution point

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.980
where GROC reaches developers and today's release directly advances the open question of model

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.640
availability and quality inside the editor. While the AI side iterates quickly, national security

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:30.860
payloads keep Falcon busy on the west coast. SpaceX successfully deployed 21 data transport satellites

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:36.920
for the space development agency on a Falcon 9 mission. The satellites form part of the

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.500
tranche wood transport layer constellation. The launch originated from Vandenberg Space Force Base.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:50.020
This marks the third such SDA mission flown by SpaceX in recent weeks. The deployment adds optical

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:56.880
cross links and resilient communications capacity for military users. Starsheald and national security

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.760
contracts remain a tracked program whose open questions center on contract awards and separation

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:09.140
from the civil constellation. Today's third tranche one flight moves that manifest forward without

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:15.580
reported delays. Another civil agency customer is also expanding Starlink's reach beyond low

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:22.240
Earth orbit. NASA selected Starlink for real-time imagery downlink from the Orion spacecraft during

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:29.300
Artemis III. The link will supplement existing deep space communications. Starlink terminals on Orion

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:34.980
will handle high bandwidth video and telemetry. The decision extends Starlink's role beyond

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:41.040
low Earth orbit services. Integration testing is expected ahead of the crude lunar mission.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.960
Starlink is a tracked program whose open questions include subscriber growth, profitability,

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.580
and direct-to-cell capability RAM. The Artemis III selection adds a new high bandwidth use case

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:59.740
that could influence both maritime and future deep space service lines. While US programs advance,

